<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mother Jones</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1837">1837</a>–<a href="/years/1930">1930</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Mary G. Harris Jones, Mary Harris Jones</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mary G. Harris Jones, known as Mother Jones from 1897, was an Irish-American labour organiser, schoolteacher and dressmaker born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cork">Cork</a>, whose family emigrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> after the Great Famine. After her husband and four children died of yellow fever in Memphis in 1867 and her shop was destroyed in the Great Chicago Fire of 1871, she organised for the Knights of Labor and the United Mine Workers of America. In 1902 she was called the most dangerous woman in America, and in 1903 she led a children's march from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> to protest weak child labour laws. She co-founded the Industrial Workers of the World and died in 1930.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mother-jones.jpg" alt="Mother Jones" width="330" height="487" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bertha Howell · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mother_Jones_1902-11-04.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
